Hi Joe,

This is a complicated issue, so I understand your confusion! however, if you look at the model in more detail described on the page you are linking:

data {
  int<lower=0> N;
  int<lower=0, upper=1> y[N];
}
parameters {
  real alpha;
}
transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0, upper=1> theta;
  theta <- inv_logit(alpha);
}
model {
  for (n in 1:N)
    y[n] ~ bernoulli(theta);
  theta ~ uniform(0, 1);
}
, you will see that theta is the transformed variable on the left side in the transformed parameters block, but also the same variable being distributed across in the model block. In this case, it is necessary to use the Jacobian adjustment. In hBayesDM, we do the following (from ra_noLA):

transformed parameters {
  vector<lower=0,upper=2>[N] rho;
  vector<lower=0>[N] tau;

  for (i in 1:N) {
    rho[i] = Phi_approx( mu_p[1] + sigma[1] * rho_p[i] ) * 2;
  }
  tau = exp( mu_p[2] + sigma[2] * tau_p );
}
model {
  # ra_prospect: Original model in Soko-Hessner et al 2009 PNAS
  # hyper parameters
  mu_p  ~ normal(0, 1.0);
  sigma ~ cauchy(0, 5.0);

  # individual parameters w/ Matt trick
  rho_p ~ normal(0, 1.0);
  tau_p ~ normal(0, 1.0);
 ...

Here, you can see that we are distributing the normal along rho_p and tau_p, and then transforming these into rho and tau. In this case, the adjustment is not necessary.

I hope this helps!

Best,
Nate

From: hbayesd...@googlegroups.com <hbayesd...@googlegroups.com> on behalf of Joseph Galaro <jga...@gmail.com>
Sent: Friday, September 29, 2017 3:28:10 PM
To: hbayesdm-users
Subject: Jacobian adjustment term for logit-transformed estimation (ra_prospect, ra_noLA, ra_noRA)

Hi all,

Thank you to the creators for putting together this great package! It's been invaluable while learning to implement STAN models.

I'm currently focused on extracting subjective preference parameters via STAN, as implemented in hBayesDM/ra_prospect. While researching the topic, I found this article (http://mc-stan.org/users/documentation/case-studies/mle-params.html) describing the importance/impact of including the Jacobian term when calculating Bayesian estimates on logit transformed variables. I'm still pretty new to STAN, but it appears the hBayesDM implementations of ra_prospect, ra_noLA, and ra_noRA don't include this term.

I'm trying to wrap my head around the validity of including the Jacobian adjustment, and would greatly appreciate if anyone could provide insight to why it is not included in the hBayesDM implementation.

Thank you for your help,
Joe

